Calculus and differential equations for dynamics — MIT Scholar courses (@mech-math-dynamics: 0, is #reference, #part-of @mech-sources) { r_3VWHWXQT3WB39Y221AV2W9AE65
[18.01SC Single Variable Calculus, Fall 2010, David Jerison](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-01sc-single-variable-calculus-fall-2010/), [18.02SC Multivariable Calculus, Fall 2010, Denis Auroux](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-02sc-multivariable-calculus-fall-2010/) and [18.03SC Differential Equations, Fall 2011](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-03sc-differential-equations-fall-2011/).

These are the mathematics courses linked by the engineering-dynamics syllabus. Use their written lessons, worked problems and assessments to develop a missing mathematical method as it becomes necessary. The indexes were inspected on 30 October 2026; this does not claim every video, applet or file was tested.

For this route, 18.01SC supplies derivatives, integrals and Taylor approximations; 18.02SC supplies vector/matrix coordinates, partial derivatives and multivariable integration; 18.03SC supplies initial-value problems, oscillators, complex forcing and systems. These courses support substantial further study of the mathematics itself. The mechanical route assigns the relevant units rather than treating enrollment in a whole degree as an entry condition.

[OCW terms](https://ocw.mit.edu/pages/privacy-and-terms-of-use/) identify CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 with component notices. Historical applet availability is not assumed. All notes, exercises, solutions and media stay at their external sources.

